  "reason": "The finding claims the database description for GEDU 501 describes a different set of trainings and requirements than what is listed on the page. However, the 'Quoted Evidence (Page)' and 'Quoted Evidence (DB)' both describe the same core requirements related to New York State teacher certification, including child abduction prevention, drug/alcohol abuse prevention, safety education, fire/arson prevention, and child abuse recognition/reporting. While the database description provides more detail about how these trainings are delivered (e.g., through BOCES, online modules) and adds a few more specific items (DASA, Title IX, ed-Law 2-d, fingerprinting, TEACH account), these are supplementary details that elaborate on the 'Issues in Health & Safety' theme rather than describing a fundamentally 'different set of trainings and requirements.' The core purpose and major components are consistent. This appears to be a false positive where additional detail is misinterpreted as a different set of requirements."
